window.LB = window.LB || {};
// worldId "p5-transport" | Plant Transport | P5 (Plant transport system)
LB["p5-transport"] = {
  notes: [
    { h: "The plant transport system", t: "A plant has two kinds of tubes that link its roots, stem and leaves: water-carrying tubes and food-carrying tubes. Together they form the plant transport system. They carry water, mineral salts and food to every living part of the plant so that it can stay alive and grow.", kw: ["water-carrying tubes", "food-carrying tubes", "transport system"] },
    { h: "Roots absorb water and mineral salts", t: "The roots absorb water and mineral salts from the soil. Mineral salts are dissolved in the water, so they enter the plant together with the water. Roots do NOT absorb food; a plant makes its own food in its leaves.", kw: ["absorb water and mineral salts", "dissolved in water"] },
    { h: "Root hairs", t: "Root hairs are tiny hair-like growths near the tips of the roots. They increase the surface area of the roots in contact with the soil, so the roots can absorb more water and mineral salts at a faster rate. Damaging the root hairs (for example when a seedling is pulled out roughly) reduces the water the plant can absorb, and it may wilt.", kw: ["root hairs", "increase the surface area", "absorb more water"] },
    { h: "Water-carrying tubes", t: "Water-carrying tubes carry water and mineral salts from the roots, up the stem, to the leaves, flowers and fruits. Water in these tubes moves only upwards, from the roots to the other parts. The leaves need this water to make food.", kw: ["carry water and mineral salts", "from the roots to all parts", "upwards"] },
    { h: "Food-carrying tubes", t: "Food (sugar) is made in the leaves. Food-carrying tubes carry this food from the leaves to all other parts of the plant, such as the stem, roots, flowers, fruits and growing tips. Food can move both up and down the plant, to wherever it is needed or stored.", kw: ["carry food made in the leaves", "to all other parts", "sugar"] },
    { h: "Why every part needs transport", t: "Every living part of a plant needs water and food to carry out life processes and grow. Parts that cannot make food, like roots, flowers and fruits, depend on the food-carrying tubes. Parts far from the soil, like leaves, depend on the water-carrying tubes.", kw: ["every living part", "needs water and food"] },
    { h: "The stem", t: "The stem holds the plant upright and holds the leaves up towards sunlight. It also contains the water-carrying and food-carrying tubes that transport substances between the roots and the leaves. Some stems also store food, such as sugar cane.", kw: ["supports the plant", "transports water and food"] },
    { h: "Veins in leaves", t: "The veins in a leaf contain water-carrying and food-carrying tubes. They bring water and mineral salts to the cells of the leaf and carry away the food made in the leaf. The veins are joined to the tubes in the stem, which are joined to the tubes in the roots.", kw: ["veins", "joined to the tubes in the stem"] },
    { h: "Where the tubes are in a stem", t: "In the stem of a flowering plant, the tubes are found in bundles. The food-carrying tubes are nearer the outside of the stem, just under the bark. The water-carrying tubes are nearer the inside (centre) of the stem.", kw: ["food-carrying tubes are nearer the outside", "just under the bark", "water-carrying tubes are nearer the inside"] },
    { h: "Coloured-water experiment", t: "When a celery stalk or a white flower is placed in red- or blue-coloured water, the coloured water travels up the water-carrying tubes. A cut across the stalk shows coloured dots only where the water-carrying tubes are, and the veins or petals turn coloured. This shows that water travels up through the water-carrying tubes to all parts.", kw: ["coloured water travels up the water-carrying tubes", "only the water-carrying tubes are stained"] },
    { h: "Ring-barking", t: "Removing a ring of bark from a stem also removes the food-carrying tubes there, but the water-carrying tubes nearer the centre are left. Food from the leaves cannot move down past the ring, so it collects just above the ring and the stem swells there. The parts below the ring, such as the roots, get no food and slowly die, and then no water is absorbed, so the whole plant eventually dies. The leaves above the ring stay fresh at first because water still reaches them.", kw: ["food-carrying tubes are removed", "swells above the ring", "roots do not receive food"] },
    { h: "Wilting and water-level experiments", t: "A plant wilts (droops) when it loses more water than it takes in, for example when its water supply is cut off. A leafy shoot in a measuring cylinder takes in water, so the water level drops; a layer of oil on the water stops water from evaporating from the water surface, so the whole drop is due to the plant. Much of the water taken in is lost as water vapour from the leaves, so a shoot with more leaves usually takes in water faster.", kw: ["wilts", "layer of oil prevents evaporation", "more leaves"] }
  ],
  traps: [
    "Roots absorb food from the soil -> roots absorb water and mineral salts; the plant makes its own food in the leaves.",
    "Water-carrying tubes carry food -> water-carrying tubes carry water and mineral salts; food-carrying tubes carry food.",
    "Food only moves down to the roots -> food-carrying tubes carry food to ALL other parts, including up to flowers, fruits and growing tips.",
    "In the coloured-water experiment both kinds of tubes turn red -> only the water-carrying tubes are stained.",
    "Ring-barking makes the leaves wilt at once -> the water-carrying tubes are still there, so the leaves above the ring stay fresh at first.",
    "The swelling forms below the ring -> food from the leaves collects just ABOVE the ring, so the swelling is above it.",
    "The oil layer stops the plant from drinking -> it stops water evaporating from the water surface, so any drop in water level is due to the plant.",
    "Root hairs hold the plant firmly in the soil -> root hairs increase the surface area for absorbing water and mineral salts."
  ],
  lessons: [
    {
      h: "Explaining the coloured-water experiment",
      concept: "In a coloured-water experiment the dye is carried with the water, so it only shows where WATER travels. The coloured dots in a cut stem are the water-carrying tubes, and coloured veins or petals show that water reached those parts. A full answer names the tube (water-carrying tubes), the direction (from the bottom of the stem up to the leaves or flower) and links it to the observation.",
      example: {
        q: "A white flower on its stem was placed in a beaker of blue-coloured water as shown. After 6 hours, the petals had turned blue. (a) Which tubes carried the blue water to the petals? (b) Explain why the petals turned blue.",
        fig: { type: "setups", items: [ { label: "A", icon: "beaker", lines: ["White flower on stem", "Blue-coloured water", "Left for 6 hours"] } ] },
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: The dye travels with the water, so ask which tubes carry water: the water-carrying tubes.",
          "Step 2: Describe the path: the blue water entered the cut end of the stem and moved UP the water-carrying tubes.",
          "Step 3: Link to the observation: the blue water reached the petals, so the petals turned blue.",
          "Step 4: Do not mention food-carrying tubes; they do not carry water."
        ],
        answer: "(a) The water-carrying tubes. (b) The blue water was carried up the stem by the water-carrying tubes to the flower, so the blue colour reached the petals and they turned blue."
      },
      tip: "Always write ‘water-carrying tubes’ in full and say where the water went FROM and TO.",
      try: {
        q: "A celery stalk was left in red-coloured water for a day. It was then cut across. Only some small dots on the cut surface were red. What were the red dots?",
        o: ["Food-carrying tubes", "Root hairs", "Water-carrying tubes", "Both water-carrying and food-carrying tubes"],
        a: 2,
        why: "The red dye moves with the water, so only the water-carrying tubes are stained. The food-carrying tubes carry food, not water, so they stay uncoloured."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Ring-barking: above the ring vs below the ring",
      concept: "Removing a ring of bark removes the food-carrying tubes, which lie just under the bark, but leaves the water-carrying tubes. Food made in the leaves cannot pass the ring, so it collects above the ring and the stem swells there. Below the ring, parts such as the roots receive no food, so they die over time, then water can no longer be absorbed and the whole plant dies.",
      example: {
        q: "Mr Tan removed a ring of bark from the main stem of a young tree. After a few weeks, the stem just above the ring had swollen. (a) Explain why the stem swelled just above the ring. (b) Explain why the whole tree died after some months.",
        fig: { type: "plant", mark: { roots: "Q", stem: "P", leaf: "R" } },
        marks: 3,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Removing the bark removes the food-carrying tubes at the ring.",
          "Step 2: Food made in the leaves (R) moves down in the food-carrying tubes but cannot pass the ring, so it collects above the ring: that is the swelling.",
          "Step 3: The roots (Q) are below the ring, so they get no food and die.",
          "Step 4: Dead roots cannot absorb water, so the leaves eventually get no water and the whole tree dies."
        ],
        answer: "(a) The food-carrying tubes were removed with the bark, so food made in the leaves could not be carried down past the ring. The food collected just above the ring, causing the swelling. (b) The roots did not receive food, so they died. The dead roots could not absorb water, so the whole tree died."
      },
      tip: "For ring-barking, state three links: food-carrying tubes removed -> food collects above ring -> roots below get no food and die.",
      try: {
        q: "A ring of bark was removed from a branch. Why did the leaves on that branch stay fresh for many weeks?",
        o: ["The leaves stopped needing water once the bark was removed", "The water-carrying tubes were not removed, so water still reached the leaves", "The food-carrying tubes were not removed, so they carried water to the leaves", "The leaves took in enough water from the damp air around them"],
        a: 1,
        why: "The water-carrying tubes are nearer the centre of the stem, so they are left when the bark is removed. Water and mineral salts still travel up to the leaves, so they do not wilt."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Reading water-level graphs and the oil layer",
      concept: "In a water-uptake experiment, the drop in water level in a measuring cylinder shows how much water the plant took in. Subtract the reading at the end from the reading at the start, and compare drops over equal time intervals to find when uptake was fastest. The layer of oil prevents water from evaporating from the water surface, so the drop is caused only by the plant.",
      example: {
        q: "A leafy shoot was placed in a measuring cylinder of water with a layer of oil on top. The graph shows the water level over 5 hours. (a) How much water did the shoot take in during the 5 hours? (b) Why was a layer of oil added?",
        fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (h)", yl: "Water level (ml)", pts: [["0", 100], ["1", 95], ["2", 90], ["3", 85], ["4", 80], ["5", 75]] },
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Read the start (100 ml) and the end (75 ml) values.",
          "Step 2: The shoot took in 100 - 75 = 25 ml of water.",
          "Step 3: The oil forms a layer over the water surface, so no water evaporates from the surface.",
          "Step 4: So the drop in water level is due only to water taken in by the shoot."
        ],
        answer: "(a) 25 ml. (b) The layer of oil prevents water from evaporating from the water surface, so that the drop in water level is due only to the water taken in by the shoot."
      },
      tip: "Always say what the oil prevents (evaporation from the water surface) AND why that matters (the drop is due only to the plant).",
      try: {
        q: "A shoot in a measuring cylinder with oil gave these water levels: 0 h: 80 ml, 1 h: 77 ml, 2 h: 70 ml, 3 h: 66 ml. During which hour did the shoot take in the most water?",
        o: ["Between 0 h and 1 h", "Between 2 h and 3 h", "Between 3 h and 4 h", "Between 1 h and 2 h"],
        a: 3,
        why: "The drops are 3 ml (0-1 h), 7 ml (1-2 h) and 4 ml (2-3 h). The largest drop, 7 ml, was between 1 h and 2 h. There is no reading at 4 h, so that option cannot be right."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Root hairs and wilting",
      concept: "Root hairs increase the surface area of the roots, so more water and mineral salts can be absorbed at a faster rate. A plant wilts when it loses more water than it absorbs, for example when its water supply is cut or its roots and root hairs are damaged. In answers, link the structure (root hairs) to the function (larger surface area) to the result (more water absorbed).",
      example: {
        q: "Siti transplanted two similar seedlings, X and Y. She pulled X out roughly, damaging many root hairs, and dug Y out carefully with the soil around its roots. The next day X had wilted but Y had not. Explain why.",
        fig: { type: "setups", items: [ { label: "X", icon: "pot", lines: ["Pulled out roughly", "Many root hairs damaged"] }, { label: "Y", icon: "pot", lines: ["Dug out with soil", "Root hairs not damaged"] } ] },
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: The only difference is the root hairs.",
          "Step 2: Fewer root hairs means a smaller surface area for absorbing water.",
          "Step 3: X absorbed less water than it lost, so it wilted; Y still absorbed enough water."
        ],
        answer: "X had fewer root hairs, so its roots had a smaller surface area to absorb water. X absorbed less water than it lost, so it wilted, while Y could still absorb enough water."
      },
      tip: "Write ‘surface area’ in full; ‘root hairs help absorb water’ alone usually gets only half the marks.",
      try: {
        q: "Why do root hairs help a plant absorb more water?",
        o: ["They increase the surface area of the roots in contact with the soil", "They make food for the roots, so the roots can grow longer", "They hold the plant firmly in the ground so it does not fall", "They carry water up the stem to the leaves of the plant"],
        a: 0,
        why: "Root hairs increase the surface area for absorbing water and mineral salts. Holding the plant firmly is a job of the whole root system, and carrying water upwards is the job of the water-carrying tubes."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Fair tests in plant transport experiments",
      concept: "To find out how one factor affects water uptake, change ONLY that factor and keep everything else the same: type and size of plant, starting volume of water, oil layer, place and time. Pick the two set-ups that differ in the factor being tested and are the same in every other way. A set-up without a plant can show whether water is lost without a plant.",
      example: {
        q: "Ali wanted to find out if the number of leaves affects the amount of water a shoot takes in. Which two set-ups should he compare? Explain your answer.",
        fig: { type: "setups", items: [ { label: "A", icon: "jar", lines: ["Shoot, 8 leaves", "Oil layer", "100 ml water"] }, { label: "B", icon: "jar", lines: ["Shoot, 4 leaves", "No oil layer", "100 ml water"] }, { label: "C", icon: "jar", lines: ["Shoot, 4 leaves", "Oil layer", "100 ml water"] }, { label: "D", icon: "jar", lines: ["No shoot", "Oil layer", "100 ml water"] } ] },
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: The factor to change is the number of leaves.",
          "Step 2: Look for two set-ups that differ ONLY in the number of leaves.",
          "Step 3: A (8 leaves) and C (4 leaves) both have an oil layer and 100 ml of water. B has no oil layer, so comparing B with A changes two things."
        ],
        answer: "A and C. They differ only in the number of leaves; both have an oil layer and the same volume of water, so it is a fair test."
      },
      tip: "Check every line of each set-up: the pair must differ in ONE thing only.",
      try: {
        q: "Using the same four set-ups (A: shoot with 8 leaves, oil; B: shoot with 4 leaves, no oil; C: shoot with 4 leaves, oil; D: no shoot, oil), which two set-ups show whether the oil layer affects the drop in water level?",
        o: ["A and B", "A and D", "C and D", "B and C"],
        a: 3,
        why: "B and C both have a shoot with 4 leaves; the only difference is the oil layer. A and B differ in both the number of leaves and the oil layer."
      }
    }
  ],
  mcq: [
    // ---------- lvl 1 (8) ----------
    { q: "The diagram shows a plant. Which part absorbs water and mineral salts from the soil?", fig: { type: "plant", mark: { roots: "C", stem: "B", leaf: "A", flower: "D" } }, o: ["A", "B", "C", "D"], a: 2, why: "C is the roots. The roots absorb water and mineral salts from the soil; the leaves make food and the stem transports substances.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "What do the water-carrying tubes of a plant carry?", o: ["Food and mineral salts", "Water and mineral salts", "Food, water and mineral salts", "Air and mineral salts"], a: 1, why: "Water-carrying tubes carry water and the mineral salts dissolved in it. Food is carried by the food-carrying tubes.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "The diagram shows a plant. In which part is food made?", fig: { type: "plant", mark: { roots: "A", stem: "B", fruit: "C", leaf: "D" } }, o: ["A", "B", "C", "D"], a: 3, why: "D is the leaf, where food is made. The fruit (C) receives food; it does not make the plant’s food.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "What do the food-carrying tubes of a plant carry?", o: ["Food made in the leaves", "Water from the roots", "Mineral salts from the soil", "Air from the leaves"], a: 0, why: "Food-carrying tubes carry food (sugar) made in the leaves to the other parts of the plant.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "How do root hairs help a plant?", o: ["They make food for the plant using sunlight", "They carry food down to the fruits and roots", "They protect the roots from insects and diseases in the soil", "They increase the surface area for absorbing water"], a: 3, why: "Root hairs increase the surface area of the roots, so more water and mineral salts can be absorbed.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "In which direction does water move in the water-carrying tubes?", o: ["From the leaves down to the roots", "From the roots up to the leaves", "From the flowers down to the leaves", "Only within the leaves"], a: 1, why: "Water is absorbed by the roots and carried up the stem to the leaves and other parts.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "Which of the following is NOT a function of the stem?", o: ["Holding the leaves up towards sunlight", "Carrying water up to the leaves", "Absorbing water from the soil", "Carrying food from the leaves to the roots"], a: 2, why: "Absorbing water from the soil is the job of the roots. The stem supports the plant and contains the tubes that transport water and food.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "A potted plant was not watered for a week on a hot balcony. Its leaves and stem drooped. What is this called?", o: ["Wilting", "Germinating", "Ring-barking", "Staining"], a: 0, why: "A plant wilts when it loses more water than it takes in. Without watering, the roots could not absorb enough water.", lvl: 1 },

    // ---------- lvl 2 (14) ----------
    { q: "A celery stalk was placed in red-coloured water as shown. After a day, it was cut across. Which parts of the cut surface would be red?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [ { label: "A", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Celery stalk", "Red-coloured water", "Left for 1 day"] } ] }, o: ["The food-carrying tubes only", "The whole cut surface", "The water-carrying tubes only", "The food-carrying and water-carrying tubes"], a: 2, why: "The red dye travels with the water, so only the water-carrying tubes are stained red. Food-carrying tubes carry food, not water.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "A white rose was placed in blue-coloured water. After some hours, its petals turned blue. What does this show?", o: ["Water is carried up to the flower by the water-carrying tubes", "Food made in the leaves is carried to the flower by the food-carrying tubes", "The petals make blue food from the water they take in", "The flower absorbs blue water vapour from the air around it"], a: 0, why: "The blue colour moved with the water up the water-carrying tubes to the petals.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "A ring of bark was removed from the trunk of a tree. After a few weeks, where would a swelling appear?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [ { label: "T", icon: "pot", lines: ["Young tree", "Ring of bark removed", "from the trunk"] } ] }, o: ["Just above the ring", "Just below the ring", "At the roots", "At the tips of the leaves"], a: 0, why: "The food-carrying tubes are removed with the bark. Food from the leaves cannot move down past the ring and collects just above it.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "A leafy shoot was placed in a measuring cylinder of water with a layer of oil. Study the graph. How much water did the shoot take in during the 4 hours?", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (h)", yl: "Water level (ml)", pts: [["0", 100], ["1", 94], ["2", 88], ["3", 82], ["4", 76]] }, o: ["76 ml", "6 ml", "100 ml", "24 ml"], a: 3, why: "The water level dropped from 100 ml to 76 ml, so 100 - 76 = 24 ml was taken in. 76 ml is the level left, not the amount taken in.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "In a water-uptake experiment, a layer of oil is poured onto the water in the measuring cylinder. What is the purpose of the oil?", o: ["To give the plant food so it takes in more water", "To prevent water from evaporating from the water surface", "To stop the plant from taking in water through its roots", "To make the water level in the cylinder easier to read"], a: 1, why: "The oil stops water evaporating from the surface, so the drop in water level is due only to water taken in by the plant.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Study the diagram. Food is carried from part R to part P. Which tubes carry it and in which direction?", fig: { type: "plant", mark: { leaf: "R", stem: "Q", roots: "P" } }, o: ["Water-carrying tubes, upwards", "Water-carrying tubes, downwards", "Food-carrying tubes, downwards", "Food-carrying tubes, upwards"], a: 2, why: "R is the leaf and P is the roots. Food made in the leaf is carried down to the roots by the food-carrying tubes.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Leafy shoots with different numbers of leaves were placed in measuring cylinders with oil for 1 day. Study the table. Which conclusion is correct?", tbl: [["Shoot", "Number of leaves", "Water taken in (ml)"], ["P", "2", "5"], ["Q", "6", "14"], ["R", "10", "23"]], o: ["The more leaves a shoot has, the less water it takes in", "The number of leaves does not affect the water taken in", "Shoot P took in the most water", "The more leaves a shoot has, the more water it takes in"], a: 3, why: "As the number of leaves increases from 2 to 10, the water taken in increases from 5 ml to 23 ml.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Study the diagram. In which parts are both water-carrying tubes and food-carrying tubes found?", fig: { type: "plant", mark: { roots: "A", stem: "B", leaf: "C" } }, o: ["B only", "A and B only", "B and C only", "A, B and C"], a: 3, why: "Both kinds of tubes run through the roots, stem and leaves (in the veins), linking all the parts.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "A plant was pulled out of the soil and left on a table. After a few hours it wilted. Why?", o: ["Its roots could no longer absorb water to replace the water lost", "Its food-carrying tubes were removed when it was pulled out", "It could no longer make food in its roots, so it had no energy", "It had too much water in its leaves, so they became heavy"], a: 0, why: "Out of the soil, the roots cannot absorb water, but the plant keeps losing water, so it wilts.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "How do mineral salts from the soil get into a plant?", o: ["They are made in the leaves", "They are absorbed by the leaves from the air", "They are carried in by the food-carrying tubes", "They are dissolved in water and absorbed by the roots"], a: 3, why: "Mineral salts are dissolved in the soil water, which is absorbed by the roots and carried up in the water-carrying tubes.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "A carrot plant stores food in its root. How does this food reach the root?", o: ["It is absorbed from the soil by the root hairs, along with water", "It is made in the leaves and carried down by the food-carrying tubes", "It is made in the root itself using sunlight, and stays there", "It is made in the leaves and carried down by the water-carrying tubes"], a: 1, why: "The root cannot make food. Food is made in the leaves and carried down to the root by the food-carrying tubes.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Study the Venn diagram. Which statement belongs in the region labelled Q?", fig: { type: "venn", a: "Water-carrying tubes", b: "Food-carrying tubes", onlyA: ["P"], both: ["Q"], onlyB: ["R"], neither: [] }, o: ["Carry mineral salts", "Found in the stem, roots and leaves", "Carry sugar made in the leaves", "Stained in a coloured-water experiment"], a: 1, why: "Both kinds of tubes are found in the stem, roots and leaves. Carrying mineral salts and being stained belong to water-carrying tubes only; carrying sugar belongs to food-carrying tubes only.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Two similar seedlings were grown in water for 1 day. Study the graph. Seedling A had many root hairs and seedling B had most of its root hairs removed. Why did A take in more water?", fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Seedling", yl: "Water taken in (ml)", bars: [["A (many root hairs)", 12], ["B (few root hairs)", 5]] }, o: ["A had a larger surface area for absorbing water", "A made more food in its roots to take in water", "A had more food-carrying tubes to carry water", "B lost no water at all, so it needed none"], a: 0, why: "Root hairs increase the surface area of the roots, so A could absorb water faster.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Which statement about the tubes in a flowering plant’s stem is correct?", o: ["The water-carrying tubes are just under the bark, nearer the outside of the stem", "Both kinds of tubes are found only at the centre of the stem, away from the bark", "The food-carrying tubes are nearer the outside of the stem than the water-carrying tubes", "There are no tubes in the stem, only in the roots and the leaves"], a: 2, why: "The food-carrying tubes lie nearer the outside, just under the bark, while the water-carrying tubes are nearer the inside. This is why ring-barking removes the food-carrying tubes.", lvl: 2 },

    // ---------- lvl 3 (12) ----------
    { q: "A ring of bark was removed from the trunk of a mango tree. The leaves stayed fresh for many weeks, but the tree died after some months. Which is the best explanation?", o: ["The leaves could not get water, so they died first and the tree died later", "The roots received no food and died, so water could no longer be absorbed", "Food collected in the roots and made them burst, so the tree died", "The water-carrying tubes were removed, so the roots died of thirst"], a: 1, why: "Only the food-carrying tubes were removed. The roots below the ring got no food and died; then no water was absorbed and the whole tree died. The fresh leaves show that the water-carrying tubes were still working.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "The stem of a white carnation was split into two halves. One half was placed in red water and the other in blue water. What would the flower look like after a day?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [ { label: "X", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Half of split stem", "Red-coloured water"] }, { label: "Y", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Other half of stem", "Blue-coloured water"] } ] }, o: ["All the petals would be purple from the mixed colours", "All the petals would stay white, as dye cannot rise", "All the petals would be red, as red is stronger", "Some petals would be red and some would be blue"], a: 3, why: "Each water-carrying tube carries water to particular parts of the flower. Tubes in the red half carry red water to some petals; tubes in the blue half carry blue water to others.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "A shoot was placed in a measuring cylinder with oil. Study the graph. During which hour did the shoot take in the most water?", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (h)", yl: "Water level (ml)", pts: [["0", 100], ["1", 96], ["2", 86], ["3", 80], ["4", 78]] }, o: ["Between 0 h and 1 h", "Between 1 h and 2 h", "Between 2 h and 3 h", "Between 3 h and 4 h"], a: 1, why: "The drops are 4, 10, 6 and 2 ml. The steepest drop (10 ml) was between 1 h and 2 h.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "A student said, “Removing a ring of bark stops water from reaching the leaves.” Which observation shows that the student is wrong?", o: ["The stem swells just above the ring after some weeks", "The roots die after some months, as they get no food", "The leaves above the ring stay fresh for many weeks", "The fruits above the ring grow bigger than usual"], a: 2, why: "If water could not reach the leaves, they would wilt quickly. They stay fresh because the water-carrying tubes are still there. The other observations are about food, not water.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Two similar trees were used. From tree X, only a ring of bark was removed. From tree Y, a deeper ring was cut, removing both the food-carrying and the water-carrying tubes. What would most likely be seen after a few days?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [ { label: "X", icon: "pot", lines: ["Ring of bark removed", "Food tubes removed"] }, { label: "Y", icon: "pot", lines: ["Deep ring cut", "Food and water tubes", "removed"] } ] }, o: ["The leaves of Y wilt, but the leaves of X stay fresh", "The leaves of both X and Y stay fresh", "The leaves of X wilt, but the leaves of Y stay fresh", "The leaves of both X and Y wilt at once"], a: 0, why: "In Y the water-carrying tubes are cut, so water cannot reach the leaves and they wilt quickly. In X the water-carrying tubes are intact, so its leaves stay fresh.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Four measuring cylinders were set up as shown in the table and left for 1 day. Based on the results, how much water did the shoot in cylinder B take in?", tbl: [["Cylinder", "Shoot", "Oil layer", "Drop in water level (ml)"], ["A", "Yes", "Yes", "20"], ["B", "Yes", "No", "26"], ["C", "No", "Yes", "0"], ["D", "No", "No", "6"]], o: ["26 ml", "6 ml", "20 ml", "32 ml"], a: 2, why: "Cylinder D shows that 6 ml evaporates from an uncovered water surface. So of the 26 ml lost from B, 26 - 6 = 20 ml was taken in by the shoot, matching A.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Which statement about food-carrying tubes is NOT true?", o: ["They carry food from the leaves to the fruits", "They are nearer the bark than the water-carrying tubes", "They are removed when a ring of bark is removed", "They carry food only downwards to the roots"], a: 3, why: "Food-carrying tubes carry food to all other parts, both up (to flowers, fruits and growing tips) and down (to the roots). The other three statements are true.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Some fruit farmers remove a thin ring of bark from a fruiting branch. The fruits on that branch, above the ring, grow bigger. Why?", o: ["More water reaches the fruits, as it can no longer flow down past the ring to the roots", "The fruits absorb more mineral salts, which are trapped above the ring in the branch", "Food made by the leaves above the ring cannot move down past it, so more food goes to the fruits", "The fruits start to make their own food, as food from the leaves cannot reach them"], a: 2, why: "The food-carrying tubes are removed at the ring, so food from the leaves on that branch stays above the ring and more of it goes to the fruits.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Study the diagram. All the leaves (C) of the plant were removed. What would most likely happen to the fruit (E) after some days?", fig: { type: "plant", mark: { roots: "A", stem: "B", leaf: "C", fruit: "E" } }, o: ["It would grow faster because it now gets all the water and minerals", "It would stop growing because no food is made and carried to it", "It would start making its own food for the rest of the plant", "It would not be affected, as it already has stored food"], a: 1, why: "Food is made in the leaves. Without leaves, no new food is made and carried by the food-carrying tubes to the fruit, so it stops growing.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "In the stem of a plant, tubes X are nearer the outside and tubes Y are nearer the inside. The stem was left in red water for a day and then cut across. Only tubes Y were stained red. Which is correct?", o: ["X carries water and mineral salts; Y carries food", "Both X and Y carry water and mineral salts", "X carries food; Y carries water and mineral salts", "Both X and Y carry food and mineral salts"], a: 2, why: "The dye travels in water, so the stained tubes Y are water-carrying tubes (nearer the inside). The unstained tubes X, nearer the outside, are the food-carrying tubes.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "A shoot was placed in a measuring cylinder with oil. After 3 hours, all its leaves were removed. Study the graph. Which statement is best supported by the graph?", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (h)", yl: "Water level (ml)", pts: [["0", 100], ["1", 94], ["2", 88], ["3", 82], ["4", 81], ["5", 80]] }, o: ["Removing the leaves reduced the amount of water taken in by the shoot", "The shoot stopped taking in any water after 3 h", "The oil layer stopped working after 3 h", "The shoot took in more water after its leaves were removed"], a: 0, why: "Before 3 h the level fell 6 ml each hour; after the leaves were removed it fell only 1 ml each hour. The shoot still took in a little water, so it did not stop completely.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Ahmad wanted to find out if root hairs affect how much water a seedling absorbs. Which two set-ups should he compare?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [ { label: "A", icon: "jar", lines: ["Seedling with", "root hairs", "Oil layer"] }, { label: "B", icon: "jar", lines: ["Root hairs removed", "No oil layer"] }, { label: "C", icon: "jar", lines: ["Root hairs removed", "Oil layer"] }, { label: "D", icon: "jar", lines: ["No seedling", "Oil layer"] } ] }, o: ["A and B", "A and D", "A and C", "B and C"], a: 2, why: "A and C differ only in whether the seedling has root hairs; both have an oil layer. A and B differ in two things (root hairs and oil).", lvl: 3 },

    // ---------- lvl 4 (6) ----------
    { q: "A branch has leaves only at its tip. Two rings of bark were removed from it: ring P just below the leaves, and ring Q further down the branch. There are no leaves between P and Q. After a few weeks, where would swelling be seen?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [ { label: "1", icon: "pot", lines: ["Leaves at tip", "Ring P below leaves", "No leaves P to Q", "Ring Q lower down"] } ] }, o: ["Just above ring P only", "Just above ring Q only", "Just above both rings", "Just below both rings"], a: 0, why: "All the food comes from the leaves at the tip. It is blocked at ring P and collects just above it. No food reaches the part between P and Q, so nothing collects above ring Q.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "A leafy shoot was left in red water for a day. A ring of bark had been removed from the middle of its stem. The stem was then cut across at three levels: above the ring, at the ring, and below the ring. At which level(s) would red dots be seen?", o: ["Below the ring only", "Above the ring only", "Below the ring and at the ring only", "At all three levels"], a: 3, why: "Removing the bark removes only the food-carrying tubes. The water-carrying tubes, nearer the centre, run unbroken through the ring, so red water travels up past it and stains them at all three levels.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Shoots with different numbers of leaves were placed in measuring cylinders with oil for 1 day. Based on the table, about how much extra water did each additional leaf cause the shoot to take in?", tbl: [["Number of leaves", "Water taken in (ml)"], ["0", "2"], ["4", "10"], ["8", "18"]], o: ["2 ml", "10 ml", "8 ml", "4 ml"], a: 0, why: "Going from 0 to 4 leaves adds 10 - 2 = 8 ml, and from 4 to 8 leaves adds another 8 ml, so each extra leaf adds 8 ÷ 4 = 2 ml. The 2 ml with 0 leaves is water taken in by the stem itself.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "A plant has two main branches, X and Y, both with many leaves. A ring of bark was removed from the base of branch X only. After many months the whole plant was still alive. Why?", o: ["Water could not pass the ring, so branch X stopped losing water", "Food made by the leaves on branch Y still reached the roots", "The roots made their own food", "The swelling on branch X stored enough water for the roots"], a: 1, why: "Food from branch X is blocked at the ring, but the food-carrying tubes of branch Y are intact, so food from Y’s leaves still reaches the roots. The roots stay alive and keep absorbing water for the whole plant.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "On a branch, there are leaves at the tip, then a fruit F, then a ring where the bark was removed, then more leaves below the ring. Which statement is correct?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [ { label: "1", icon: "pot", lines: ["Leaves at tip", "Fruit F", "Ring of bark removed", "Leaves below ring"] } ] }, o: ["F stops growing because it is cut off from all food by the ring", "F wilts because no water can pass the ring to reach it", "F keeps growing because food from the leaves at the tip reaches it", "F gets all its food from the leaves below the ring instead"], a: 2, why: "F is above the ring, on the same side as the tip leaves. Their food reaches F without passing the ring. Food from the leaves below the ring cannot cross the ring upwards, and water still passes the ring in the water-carrying tubes.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Two identical leafy celery stalks were placed in the same amount of red water in the same room. Stalk J kept all its leaves. All the leaves of stalk K were removed. After 2 hours, the red colour had travelled higher up J than up K. Which statement best explains this?", o: ["K had no water-carrying tubes left, so the red water could not rise in it", "Water is lost from the leaves, so J drew up water faster through its water-carrying tubes", "The leaves of J made the red dye, so more red colour appeared in J", "The food-carrying tubes of J carried the dye up faster than its water-carrying tubes"], a: 1, why: "Much of the water taken in is lost from the leaves, and this draws more water up the water-carrying tubes. With its leaves, J took up water faster, so the dye rose higher. K still has water-carrying tubes; they just carry water up more slowly.", lvl: 4 }
  ],
  tf: [
    { s: "Roots absorb food from the soil.", a: false, why: "Roots absorb water and mineral salts; food is made in the leaves." },
    { s: "Water-carrying tubes carry water and mineral salts from the roots to the leaves.", a: true, why: "Water with dissolved mineral salts travels up from the roots to all parts." },
    { s: "Food-carrying tubes carry food only from the leaves down to the roots.", a: false, why: "They carry food to all other parts, including up to flowers, fruits and growing tips." },
    { s: "Root hairs increase the surface area of the roots for absorbing water.", a: true, why: "More surface area in contact with the soil means more water and mineral salts can be absorbed." },
    { s: "In a coloured-water experiment, the food-carrying tubes turn red.", a: false, why: "Only the water-carrying tubes are stained, because the dye moves with the water." },
    { s: "The food-carrying tubes in a stem are nearer the outside than the water-carrying tubes.", a: true, why: "Food-carrying tubes lie just under the bark; water-carrying tubes are nearer the inside." },
    { s: "After ring-barking, the stem swells just below the ring.", a: false, why: "Food collects just ABOVE the ring because it cannot move down past it." },
    { s: "After ring-barking, the leaves above the ring wilt within a few hours.", a: false, why: "The water-carrying tubes are not removed, so water still reaches the leaves and they stay fresh at first." },
    { s: "A plant wilts when it loses more water than it takes in.", a: true, why: "Without enough water, the plant cannot stay firm, so it droops." },
    { s: "The oil layer in a water-uptake experiment stops the plant from taking in water.", a: false, why: "It stops water evaporating from the water surface, so the drop in water level is due only to the plant." },
    { s: "Mineral salts enter the plant dissolved in water.", a: true, why: "The roots absorb soil water containing dissolved mineral salts." },
    { s: "The veins of a leaf contain water-carrying and food-carrying tubes.", a: true, why: "The veins bring water to the leaf cells and carry food away from the leaf." },
    { s: "Water moves both up and down in the water-carrying tubes.", a: false, why: "Water in the water-carrying tubes moves upwards, from the roots to the other parts." },
    { s: "A ring-barked tree eventually dies because its roots receive no food.", a: true, why: "The roots die without food, and then no water is absorbed, so the whole tree dies." },
    { s: "The fruits of a plant make the food that is stored in them.", a: false, why: "The food is made in the leaves and carried to the fruits by the food-carrying tubes." },
    { s: "Roots cannot make their own food, so they depend on the food-carrying tubes.", a: true, why: "Food made in the leaves is carried down to the roots." },
    { s: "Removing a ring of bark also removes the water-carrying tubes.", a: false, why: "The water-carrying tubes are nearer the centre of the stem and are left behind." },
    { s: "A seedling whose root hairs were damaged may wilt after it is transplanted.", a: true, why: "With fewer root hairs, less water is absorbed, so it may lose more water than it takes in." },
    { s: "Food is carried in a plant in the form of mineral salts.", a: false, why: "Mineral salts come from the soil and are not food; the food carried is sugar made in the leaves." },
    { s: "A leafy shoot usually takes in water faster than the same shoot with its leaves removed.", a: true, why: "Much of the water is lost from the leaves, which draws more water up the shoot." }
  ],
  sort: [
    { title: "Sort these into what each kind of tube does or is", groups: ["Water-carrying tubes", "Food-carrying tubes"], items: [["Carry mineral salts", 0], ["Carry sugar", 1], ["Stained in coloured water", 0], ["Removed in ring-barking", 1], ["Carry substances from roots upwards", 0], ["Carry food to the roots", 1], ["Nearer the centre of the stem", 0], ["Just under the bark", 1], ["Carry food to the fruits", 1]] },
    { title: "Sort each job into the plant part that does it", groups: ["Roots", "Stem", "Leaves"], items: [["Absorb water from the soil", 0], ["Have root hairs", 0], ["Absorb mineral salts", 0], ["Hold the leaves up to the light", 1], ["Link the roots to the leaves", 1], ["Make food", 2], ["Have veins that bring water to their cells", 2], ["Hold the plant upright", 1]] },
    { title: "A ring of bark is removed from a tree trunk. Sort each result by where it happens", groups: ["Above the ring", "Below the ring"], items: [["Food collects here", 0], ["The stem swells here", 0], ["The leaves stay fresh at first", 0], ["The roots get no food", 1], ["Parts slowly starve", 1], ["Fruits may grow bigger", 0], ["Root hairs die and stop absorbing water", 1], ["Food is still made here", 0]] }
  ],
  oe: [
    { q: "What is the function of the water-carrying tubes in a plant?", marks: 2, kw: [["water and mineral", "water and dissolved mineral", "mineral salts"], ["from the roots", "roots to"]], model: "The water-carrying tubes carry water and mineral salts from the roots to all parts of the plant." },
    { q: "Why do roots have root hairs?", marks: 2, kw: [["surface area"], ["absorb more water", "more water", "absorb water"]], model: "Root hairs increase the surface area of the roots, so the roots can absorb more water and mineral salts." },
    { q: "A celery stalk was placed in red-coloured water for a day, as shown. Its leaves turned slightly red. The stalk was then cut across, and only some small dots on the cut surface were red. (a) Name the tubes that carried the red water. (b) Explain why the leaves turned red. (c) Why did the rest of the cut surface of the stalk NOT turn red?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [ { label: "A", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Celery with leaves", "Red-coloured water", "Left for 1 day"] } ] }, marks: 3, kw: [["water-carrying", "water carrying"], ["up the stalk", "carried up", "travelled up", "moved up", "up to the leaves"], ["food-carrying", "food carrying", "other parts", "only the water"]], model: "(a) The water-carrying tubes. (b) The red water was carried up the stalk by the water-carrying tubes to the veins of the leaves, so the leaves turned red. (c) Only the water-carrying tubes carry the red water; the food-carrying tubes and other parts do not carry water up the stalk, so they are not stained." },
    { q: "Mr Lim removed a ring of bark from the trunk of a rambutan tree, as shown. (a) What tubes were removed together with the bark? (b) After a few weeks, the trunk just above the ring had swollen. Explain why. (c) Explain why the tree died after some months.", fig: { type: "plant", mark: { stem: "Ring", leaf: "Leaves", roots: "Roots" } }, marks: 4, kw: [["food-carrying", "food carrying"], ["cannot be carried down", "could not be carried down", "cannot move down", "could not move down", "cannot pass", "could not pass", "collected above", "collects above"], ["roots did not receive food", "roots do not receive food", "roots received no food", "roots get no food", "roots got no food", "no food reach", "roots died", "roots die"], ["absorb water", "no water", "absorbed"]], model: "(a) The food-carrying tubes. (b) Food made in the leaves could not move down past the ring, so the food collected above the ring and the trunk swelled there. (c) The roots received no food, so the roots died. The dead roots could not absorb water, so the whole tree died." },
    { q: "A leafy shoot was placed in a measuring cylinder of water with a layer of oil. The graph shows the water level. (a) How much water did the shoot take in over the 4 hours? (b) What was the purpose of the oil layer? (c) Why is it important?", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (h)", yl: "Water level (ml)", pts: [["0", 60], ["1", 56], ["2", 52], ["3", 48], ["4", 44]] }, marks: 3, kw: [["16"], ["evaporat"], ["due only", "only due", "only because", "taken in by the shoot", "taken in by the plant"]], model: "(a) 16 ml. (b) The oil layer prevents water from evaporating from the water surface. (c) So the drop in water level is due only to the water taken in by the shoot." },
    { q: "A potted balsam plant on a sunny corridor was not watered for several days. Its leaves drooped. Explain why.", marks: 2, kw: [["loses more water", "lost more water", "more water than"], ["not enough water", "less water", "no water", "absorbed less"]], model: "The soil became dry, so the roots absorbed less water. The plant lost more water than it took in, so the plant wilted." },
    { q: "Mrs Ng pulled a seedling out of the soil roughly and replanted it. The next day it had wilted, although the soil was wet. (a) Which part of the roots was most likely damaged? (b) Explain why the seedling wilted.", fig: { type: "setups", items: [ { label: "S", icon: "pot", lines: ["Seedling replanted", "Pulled out roughly", "Soil kept wet"] } ] }, marks: 3, kw: [["root hair"], ["surface area"], ["less water", "absorb less", "absorbed less", "lost more water"]], model: "(a) The root hairs. (b) With fewer root hairs, the surface area of the roots for absorbing water was smaller, so the roots absorbed less water than the seedling lost, and it wilted." },
    { q: "Sweet potato plants store food in their roots. Explain how the food gets into the roots.", marks: 2, kw: [["made in the leaves", "leaves make", "made by the leaves"], ["food-carrying", "food carrying"]], model: "The food is made in the leaves and carried down to the roots by the food-carrying tubes." },
    { q: "The stem of a white flower was split into two halves. One half was put in blue water and the other in red water. After a day, some petals were blue and some were red. (a) Which tubes carried the coloured water? (b) Explain why the petals were not all the same colour.", fig: { type: "setups", items: [ { label: "X", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Half of split stem", "Blue-coloured water"] }, { label: "Y", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Other half of stem", "Red-coloured water"] } ] }, marks: 3, kw: [["water-carrying", "water carrying"], ["each half", "each tube", "different tubes", "separate tubes", "particular"], ["some petals", "different petals", "different parts"]], model: "(a) The water-carrying tubes. (b) The water-carrying tubes in each half of the stem carried water to particular parts of the flower, so blue water reached some petals and red water reached different petals." },
    { q: "Study the table. Four measuring cylinders were left for 1 day. (a) How much water evaporated from the uncovered water surface in D? (b) Using the results, how much water did the shoot in B take in? (c) Explain your answer to (b).", tbl: [["Cylinder", "Shoot", "Oil layer", "Drop in water level (ml)"], ["A", "Yes", "Yes", "18"], ["B", "Yes", "No", "25"], ["C", "No", "Yes", "0"], ["D", "No", "No", "7"]], marks: 3, kw: [["7"], ["18"], ["evaporat"]], model: "(a) 7 ml. (b) 18 ml. (c) The 25 ml drop in B includes 7 ml of water that evaporated from the uncovered water surface, as shown by D, so the shoot took in 25 - 7 = 18 ml, the same as the shoot in A.", x: 1 },
    { q: "Fruit farmers sometimes remove a thin ring of bark from a branch that is bearing fruit. The fruits on that branch grow bigger. (a) Which tubes are removed? (b) Explain why the fruits grow bigger.", marks: 3, kw: [["food-carrying", "food carrying"], ["cannot move down", "cannot be carried down", "cannot pass", "stays above", "remains above"], ["more food"]], model: "(a) The food-carrying tubes. (b) Food made in the leaves on that branch cannot move down past the ring, so it stays above the ring and more food is carried to the fruits, which grow bigger." },
    { q: "Study the diagram. In the stem of a plant, tube X lies just under the bark and tube Y lies nearer the centre. (a) Name X and Y. (b) A ring of bark was removed from the stem. Why did the leaves above the ring NOT wilt?", fig: { type: "plant", mark: { stem: "X and Y", leaf: "Leaf", roots: "Roots" } }, marks: 3, kw: [["x is the food", "x food", "x is a food", "x are food", "x is food"], ["y is the water", "y water", "y is a water", "y are water", "y is water"], ["water still", "still carry", "still reach", "still travel", "still be carried"]], model: "(a) X is the food-carrying tube and Y is the water-carrying tube. (b) Only the food-carrying tubes were removed with the bark. The water-carrying tubes were left, so water could still be carried up to the leaves, and the leaves did not wilt." },
    { q: "A plant has two branches, P and Q, both with many leaves. A ring of bark was removed from the base of branch P only, as shown. (a) Where on branch P would a swelling appear? (b) Explain why the roots stay alive. (c) Explain why the leaves on branch P stay fresh.", fig: { type: "setups", items: [ { label: "P", icon: "pot", lines: ["Branch with leaves", "Ring of bark removed", "at its base"] }, { label: "Q", icon: "pot", lines: ["Branch with leaves", "No ring removed"] } ] }, marks: 3, kw: [["above the ring"], ["branch q", "leaves on q", "from q", "q's leaves", "q s leaves"], ["water-carrying", "water carrying"]], model: "(a) Just above the ring. (b) Food made by the leaves on branch Q is still carried down to the roots by its food-carrying tubes, so the roots receive food. (c) The water-carrying tubes of branch P were not removed, so water still reaches its leaves.", x: 1 },
    { q: "Two identical leafy celery stalks were placed in equal amounts of red water. All the leaves of stalk K were removed; stalk J kept its leaves. After 2 hours, the red colour had risen higher in J. (a) Which tubes carried the red water? (b) Explain why the red colour rose higher in J. (c) State one variable that must be kept the same for a fair test.", marks: 3, kw: [["water-carrying", "water carrying"], ["lost from the leaves", "loses water", "lose water", "water is lost", "faster", "more water"], ["amount of red water", "same amount", "same room", "same place", "same size", "same type", "same length", "temperature", "same time"]], model: "(a) The water-carrying tubes. (b) Water is lost from the leaves of J, so J took up more water faster through its water-carrying tubes and the red colour rose higher. (c) The same amount of red water.", x: 1 }
  ],
  doc: [
    { q: "Explain why a ring-barked tree swells just above the ring.", marks: 2, answers: ["Because the bark is removed so the tree is hurt and swells.", "The food-carrying tubes are removed, so food from the leaves cannot move down past the ring and collects above it.", "Water cannot go up past the ring, so it collects there."], best: 1, why: "Answer 2 names the food-carrying tubes and explains that food collects above the ring. Answer 1 is vague (‘hurt’) with no scientific idea. Answer 3 is wrong: the water-carrying tubes are not removed, and water moves upward, so it would not collect above the ring." },
    { q: "Why is a layer of oil added to the water in a measuring cylinder holding a leafy shoot?", marks: 2, answers: ["To prevent water from evaporating from the water surface, so the drop in water level is due only to the shoot.", "So that the water does not evaporate.", "To stop the plant from taking in too much water."], best: 0, why: "Answer 1 gives both what the oil does and why it matters. Answer 2 gives only half the idea: it does not say why stopping evaporation is important. Answer 3 is a misconception; the oil does not affect the plant’s uptake." },
    { q: "Why do root hairs help a plant take in water?", marks: 2, answers: ["They help the roots drink water.", "They hold the plant firmly so it can take in water.", "They increase the surface area of the roots, so more water can be absorbed."], best: 2, why: "Answer 3 uses the key idea ‘increase the surface area’ and links it to more water absorbed. Answer 1 is too vague (‘help… drink’). Answer 2 gives the wrong function; holding the plant firmly is not the job of root hairs." },
    { q: "A white flower was placed in blue water and its petals turned blue. Explain why.", marks: 2, answers: ["The flower likes the blue water.", "The blue water was carried up the stem by the water-carrying tubes to the petals.", "The blue water was carried up by the food-carrying tubes to the petals."], best: 1, why: "Answer 2 names the correct tubes and the path of the water. Answer 1 gives no science. Answer 3 names the wrong tubes; food-carrying tubes carry food, not water." },
    { q: "A ring of bark was removed from a tree trunk. Explain why the tree eventually died.", marks: 2, answers: ["The roots did not get food, so they died and could no longer absorb water for the tree.", "The tree lost its bark, so it died.", "The leaves could not get water after the bark was removed, so the tree died."], best: 0, why: "Answer 1 links no food to the roots, the roots dying, and no water absorbed. Answer 2 only repeats the question. Answer 3 is wrong: the water-carrying tubes were not removed, so the leaves still got water at first." }
  ]
};
